<h1>Entity Types</h1>
<hr align="left" width="100.526%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p>Every entity belongs to a particular data type. Here follows a list describing the types available in KUBE. All data types are supported by the native <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>SAB2</b></span> format. The native <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>SAB</b></span> format supports all of the entity types with the exception of Current CS entity, cones, rectangular and round slots.</p>
<table width="811" cellspacing="0" class="whs4">
<col class="whs5">
<col class="whs6">
<col class="whs7">
<tr valign="top" class="whs8">
<td bgcolor="#EAE8D6" width="15.647%" class="whs9">
<p class="caption">Entity Type</p>
</td>
<td bgcolor="#EAE8D6" width="77.24%" class="whs10">
<p class="caption">Description</p>
</td>
<td bgcolor="#EAE8D6" width="7.112%" class="whs11">
<p class="caption">Info</p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr valign="top" class="whs8">
<td width="15.647%" class="whs12">
<p class="normal-table-func">Scan data</p>
</td>
<td width="77.24%" class="whs13">
<p class="normal-table">The raw point data gathered by the sensor. Each scan consists of stripes arranged in one or more patches. The scan data is usually more dense than actually required.</p>
</td>
<td width="7.112%" class="whs14">
<p class="normal-table"><a href="Scan_Data_Description.htm"><img src="../images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs15" alt=""></a></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr valign="top" class="whs8">
<td width="15.647%" class="whs12">
<p class="normal-table-func">Meshes</p>
</td>
<td width="77.24%" class="whs13">
<p class="normal-table">They are created from scan data by merging and polygon reduction.</p>
</td>
<td width="7.112%" class="whs14">
<p class="normal-table"><a href="Mesh_Description.htm"><img src="../images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs15" alt=""></a></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr valign="top" class="whs8">
<td width="15.647%" class="whs12">
<p class="normal-table-func">Point clouds</p>
</td>
<td width="77.24%" class="whs13">
<p class="normal-table">They differ from a scan in that the points are not organised into stripes and patches. A point cloud is created by volume sampling. Special cases of point clouds are:</p>
</td>
<td width="7.112%" class="whs14">
<p class="normal-table"><a href="Point_Clouds_Description.htm"><img src="../images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs15" alt=""></a></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr valign="top" class="whs8">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="15.647%" class="whs12">
<p class="normal-table-func">Probe points</p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="77.24%" class="whs13">
<p class="normal-table">Captured by the localiser and the probe.</p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="7.112%" class="whs14">
<p class="normal-table"><a href="Probe_Points.htm"><img src="../images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs15" alt=""></a></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr valign="top" class="whs8">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="15.647%" class="whs12">
<p class="normal-table-func">Static stripes</p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="77.24%" class="whs13">
<p class="normal-table">Captured by the localiser and the sensor.</p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="7.112%" class="whs14">
<p class="normal-table"><a href="Static_Stripes.htm"><img src="../images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs15" alt=""></a></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr valign="top" class="whs8">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="15.647%" class="whs12">
<p class="normal-table-func">Polylines</p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="77.24%" class="whs13">
<p class="normal-table">They are commonly used as a means of passing information to a CAD system for surface construction.</p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="7.112%" class="whs14">
<p class="normal-table"><a href="Polylines_Description.htm"><img src="../images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs15" alt=""></a></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr valign="top" class="whs8">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="15.647%" class="whs12">
<p class="normal-table-func">Datums</p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="77.24%" class="whs13">
<p class="normal-table">They are used to set up reference systems. The use of datum entities together with the localiser probe allows the user to scan in bodyline co-ordinates.</p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="7.112%" class="whs14">
<p class="normal-table"><a href="datums_description.htm"><img src="../images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs15" alt=""></a></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr valign="top" class="whs8">
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="15.647%" class="whs16">
<p class="normal-table-func">Transformations</p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="77.24%" class="whs17">
<p class="normal-table"><span lang="EN-US">The transformations are used to align existing entities to a known position or to scan directly in a new co-ordinate system. There is a special transformation called</span> <span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Current CS</b></span><span lang="EN-US">. It is an e</span>ntity that contains the currently active co-ordinate system. It can be World CS or any transformation.</p>
</td>
<td colspan="1" rowspan="1" width="7.112%" class="whs14">
<p class="normal-table"><a href="Transformation_Description.htm"><img src="../images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs15" alt=""></a></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr valign="top" class="whs8">
<td width="15.647%" class="whs16">
<p class="normal-table-func">Viewport Positions</p>
</td>
<td width="77.24%" class="whs17">
<p class="normal-table">They are used to save a particular position and orientation of the viewport according to the global co-ordinate system.</p>
</td>
<td width="7.112%" class="whs14">
<p class="normal-table"><a href="../../References/Create_Viewport_Entity.htm"><img src="../images/i_blue_sm.jpg" width="23px" height="16px" border="0" class="img_whs15" alt=""></a></p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
